                    },
                    {
                      "type": "rectangle",
                      "id": "TiqKu",
                      "name": "Hairline",
                      "fill": "$brand-rule",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "height": 1
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "oRyrs",
                      "name": "Etymology note",
                      "fill": "$brand-ink-soft",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "slipstream, n.  Aeronautics, 1913: the stream of air driven astern by a propeller, and the body of air dragged behind any moving craft. Cycling, mid-century: the pocket of low pressure a rider leaves behind, in which a follower holds the same speed on roughly a third less work. Both senses keep one bargain — the one in front breaks the air, the one behind keeps the pace and spends less. The name is taken in the second sense and claims nothing beyond it.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.6,
                      "fontFamily": "$font-body",
                      "fontSize": 13,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
